AD BROCHURE FOR TUTTLE CONSTRUCTION

What We Need
We're putting together a print brochure to hand out at regional business events and trade shows. Nothing crazy flashy, just something solid that shows what we do and why people should trust us with their projects. This thing's going to represent the company at meetings with potential clients, so it needs to look professional and actually say something useful.

About Us
Tuttle Construction has been around since 1928. Yeah, you read that right - over 95 years of building stuff in Ohio. We're based in Lima with another office in Columbus. Most of our work is commercial and industrial: hospitals, factories, schools, that kind of thing. We belong to Tuttle Services, which is our parent company. Lima office sits at 880 Shawnee Road, Lima, OH 45805. Columbus is at 2800 Corporate Exchange Dr., Columbus, OH 43231 if you need to reach either location.

What This Brochure Should Do
Target audience: business owners, project managers, people who make decisions about construction work. We're talking medium to large projects here, not residential stuff. The brochure needs to show our capabilities, list some of our better projects, and get across that we're the kind of company that actually delivers what we promise.

Format
Tri-fold works best. It needs to fit in a standard business envelope so we can mail it without any weird folding or cramming. Keep standard print margins and bleeds in mind - nothing that costs extra at the print shop.

What's Supposed to Be in It
1. Who we are - a quick history and what we're about
2. What we do - general contracting, concrete, steel, masonry, metal buildings, fireproofing
3. Projects we've done - Ford facilities, hospitals, schools, that sort of thing. We won the Build Ohio Award seven times, so mention that
4. How we work - solution-based, confident, supportive, honest
5. Where to find us - both offices and our website
6. Company logo up top or down bottom, somewhere it makes sense

Look and Feel
Colors: navy blue and orange. Not neon orange, more like a warm but professional shade. Think of it as the kind of colors a company that's been around since the 20s would actually use. Plenty of white space so it doesn't look cluttered.

Fonts: bold sans-serif for headings, something clean for the body. Keep it readable when it's printed out. Don't go below 14pt for headings or 9pt for body text.

Photos: we'll send you actual shots of our projects. No stock images, they want the real deal. Position them so they support the text without drowning it out.

What You Deliver
Final files should be print-ready PDFs with CMYK colors, proper bleeds, 300 DPI minimum. Also hand over the source files so we can make changes later if needed - Illustrator, InDesign, whatever you prefer. All fonts need to be outlined or embedded.

How We Work
We do a first look, then a couple rounds of tweaks if something doesn't sit right. Anything beyond that, we might need to talk about extra costs. Marketing has to sign off before things go to print.

A Few Other Things
Make sure there's no licensing issues with anything you include. You keep the creative rights until we pay up. During review we might tweak some wording here and there. If this goes well, there could be more work down the line - other brochures, flyers, whatever comes up.

Reach Out
Questions go to the department email. Everything starts over email so we have a record of what we discussed and agreed on.


